---
/*
  /reading — the long-form typography bench: Newsreader judged at real essay
  length, in the measure and rhythm the published page uses. Working scaffolding
  for building the site, not a room a reader is meant to find.

  ⚠ DEV-GATED 2026-08-09, and this one was worse than the four gated before it.
  /styleguide and the three `*-lab` benches showed primitives; THIS page ships a
  FABRICATED ESSAY — an invented "Forgiveness" with invented constellations and
  an invented date — public, un-gated and indexable, one route away from the
  real essay of that name at /blog/forgiveness. A search engine had no way to
  tell which was the writing.

  It predates the 2026-08-07 sweep, whose comment in styleguide.astro shows it
  only knew about four bench pages. Five then answered "is this public?" the
  same way instead of four answering it one way and one drifting.

  ⚠ AND IT IS ONE OF THE TWO THAT SURVIVED the 2026-08-10 deletion (plan 31 §7),
  for the reason its own paragraph below already gives: sky-lab, reveal-lab and
  afford-lab each existed to answer ONE question and were deleted once it was
  answered. A type reference has no such question — which is the same argument
  that kept /styleguide.

  ⚠ THE ALTERNATIVE THAT LOST WAS DELETING IT, and the case was real:
  `blog/[slug]` + PostArticle render every element this prototypes — `.reading`,
  `.reading-lead`, `prose-measure`, the blockquote, the h2s — against fifty real
  essays, so the layout's true consumer has absorbed it. What kept the file is
  that a real essay is CONTENT and this is a CONTROL: judging a type change here
  costs one page load and touches nothing that is published. Reverting the gate
  is one line if that ever stops being worth 103 lines of sample prose.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Timestamp from '../components/Timestamp.astro';
import Label from '../components/Label.astro';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

// Sample content — a plausible essay, so we can judge Newsreader at real length.
// ⚠ Fabricated, every word of it. See the header: this is why the page is gated.
const constellations = ['conditions, not character', 'the invisible graveyard'];
---

<Base title="Forgiveness — It Only Happens Once">
  <main class="px-6 py-16 sm:py-24">
    <article class="prose-measure mx-auto">
      <!-- which constellations this piece lives in -->
      <div class="text-base-content/45 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 font-serif text-sm">
        {
          constellations.map((l) => (
            <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
              <span class="text-primary">✦</span>
              {l}
            </span>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h1 class="font-display mt-6 text-4xl leading-[1.1] font-light sm:text-6xl">Forgiveness</h1>

      <div class="text-base-content/40 mt-6 flex items-center gap-3 text-sm">
        <Timestamp date="2023-05-31" />
        <span class="font-sans">·</span>
        <span class="font-sans">6 min read</span>
      </div>

      <div class="reading mt-14">
        <p class="reading-lead">
          For most of my life I believed forgiveness was a decision — a door you chose to open, a generosity you
          extended by force of will. I don't believe that anymore.
        </p>

        <p>
          What changed wasn't my heart. It was my model of people. Somewhere along the way I started tracing behavior
          back to its causes — really tracing it, past the point where blame is comfortable — and I kept arriving at the
          same place: <em>conditions</em>. The person who hurt me was, in almost every case, doing the only thing their
          history had equipped them to do. Not excused. Explained.
        </p>

        <p>
          And explanation, it turns out, does something that willpower never could. When you follow the full causal
          chain behind an action, the
          <em>agent</em> you wanted to blame quietly dissolves. There is no one standing at the center of it who could have
          done otherwise. What's left isn't anger. It's a kind of tired tenderness.
        </p>

        <blockquote>The best revenge is not to be like your enemy.</blockquote>

        <h2>What understanding does</h2>

        <p>
          I used to think this made me soft — that refusing to hold a grudge was a failure of self-respect. I've come to
          think the opposite. Resentment assumes a free agent who <strong>chose</strong> to wrong you. Remove the assumption
          and resentment has nothing to hold onto; it isn't suppressed, it's <em>unfounded</em>. That's not weakness.
          It's just accuracy.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a cost to seeing this way, and I won't pretend there isn't. If no one is finally to blame, then some
          part of you has to carry the grief without the relief of having someone to hand it to. The pain was real. It
          simply didn't have an author.
        </p>

        <h2>The part I keep having to relearn</h2>

        <p>
          The strange thing is how hard it is to extend the same mercy inward. I can forgive almost anyone their
          conditions — except the younger version of myself, who by the exact same logic could not have done otherwise
          either. The framework that sets everyone else free keeps making an exception for me. I'm still
          <a href="#">working on that</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          But that is the direction, at least. Forgiveness stopped being something I <em>do</em> and became something I <em
            >see</em
          > — and once you see it, you can't quite unsee it. It only happens once, this life, and most of the people in it
          are trying about as hard as their conditions allow.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- where it lives -->
      <footer class="border-base-300 mt-20 border-t pt-8">
        <Label variant="voice">This piece lives in</Label>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-8 gap-y-2 text-xl">
          {
            constellations.map((l) => (
              <a href="#" class="hover:text-primary inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-light transition-colors">
                <span class="text-primary">✦</span>
                {l}
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
